In order to reconstruct the vector \(p_{T}\) sum of all reconstructed final-state objects referred to as \(\vec{p}_{T}^{\;\;balance}\), the \(p_{T}\) of the candidate track and \(p_{T}\) of all the jets whose thrust axes1 are in the away-side of the near-side cone as shown in orange color region in Figure 7.7 were considered. These jets were reconstructed using a standard algorithm known as the anti-\(k_{T}\)[114] algorithm2 which is widely used in the reconstruction of particle jets in high energy particle collision experiments. The parameters used for the jet reconstruction at the STAR experiment cited above are listed in the Appendix B. Next, the \(\vec{p}_{T}^{\;\;balance}\) is defined as the vector sum of the \(e^{\pm}\) candidate's \(p_{T}\) vector, \(\vec{p}_{T}^{\;\;e}\) and the \(p_{T}\) vectors of all reconstructed jets mentioned above, \(\vec{p}_{T}^{\;\;jets}\) as shown in Equation37.1.

