#### Systematic Uncertainty of \(A_{l}\)

As mentioned before, the systematic uncertainty on the measured \(A_{L}\) due to the background dilution factor \(\beta\) were included in the total statistical uncertainty of the \(A_{L}\) that were listed in the table 9.4 and are on the order of 5% of the total statistical uncertainty.

The systematic uncertainty on \(A_{L}\) due to the relative luminosity correction discussed in Sec. 9.2 was estimated. This was accomplished by selecting an another statistically independent set of QCD background sample, similar to the one used to obtain the relative luminosity correction factors themselves. Similarly, these QCD events were selected from the same sample that was triggered for W and high-\(p_{T}\) track requirements. However, the conditions of isolation requirements discussed in Sec. 7.3.3 and signed-Pt balance requirement discussed in Sec. 7.4.1 were oppositely imposed in order to select only QCD type events. Next, only the events in the \(E_{T}\) range of 25 to 50 GeV from this sample was used and their single-spin asymmetries, \(A_{L}^{QCD\pm}\) were calculated in a single bin by combining all 4 STAR-\(\eta\) bins together. This \(A_{L}^{QCD\pm}\) was calculated in the same way where W \(A_{L}\) was calculated except \(\beta\) obviously was set to zero. The values are given in the table 9.5 along with their statistical uncertainties.

